qEtNAWM\";}s:12:\"reviews_data\";a:5:{i:0;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:19:\"TIMOTHY LOI SOSMENA\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:125:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjVz0rApefGC2sbMdE_yGC-KadFC6dpkzLmoYJYLOKZWVX7tGsFOnw=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba6\";s:6:\"rating\";i:4;s:4:\"text\";s:1259:\"One of Manila’s most historically significant and emotionally resonant landmarks, this bridge stands as a graceful reminder of the city’s colonial past. Its 19th-century Beaux-Arts design lends it a distinctly European character, yet the atmosphere surrounding it is unmistakably Filipino—a compelling juxtaposition of imported architecture and local urban life.\n\nI walked here on the morning of May 9, 2026. At that early hour, the bridge was remarkably quiet, with only a few passing vehicles and almost no pedestrians. The usual evening stalls had not yet been set up, giving the area a more subdued and contemplative mood.\n\nThe esplanade surrounding the bridge is beautifully laid out and surprisingly well-maintained. It has recently gained popularity, especially among younger people on social media, and it’s easy to see why. The restored walkways and open views of the river create a picturesque setting, particularly in the softer light of morning.\n\nThe only notable drawback is the persistent odor from the Pasig River, which can occasionally detract from the otherwise pleasant experience.\n\nOne of Manila’s most evocative public spaces—a place where history, architecture, and contemporary city life converge in a uniquely Filipino way.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-05-09T01:39:05.085804929Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:1;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:14:\"Doreen Bendong\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:125:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjUOhJhynKgie8yoQW0ee4IdgKnKHidASveP_Gm33aIiCfUEnE1pNQ=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba2\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:694:\"Jones Bridge is one of our go-to spots whenever we want to enjoy a nice view while grabbing something to eat. The area has plenty of affordable food options, making it a great place for a quick and budget-friendly trip.\n\nOne of the best things about visiting Jones Bridge is that it\'s close to other popular attractions. You can easily explore Chinatown and visit Fort Santiago if you have extra time. The entrance fee for Fort Santiago is ₱75 for regular visitors and ₱50 for students, making it an affordable historical destination.\n\nOverall, Jones Bridge is a great place to relax, enjoy the scenery, and experience a little bit of Manila\'s culture and history without spending too much.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-06-09T14:39:23.873194342Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:2;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:11:\"Sobi Thomas\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:123:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjVaNr7XFddvPvifGX8R1xAQKjL7GQO_BjJObUxSjW8veoQkCzIs=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:577:\"Passed through  during our visit to Manila and enjoyed the scenic views of the historic Pasig River and the surrounding cityscape. The bridge serves as an important landmark connecting different parts of the city and provides access to the vibrant district of Binondo, widely recognized as the world\'s oldest Chinatown.\n\nThe elegant design of the bridge, especially when illuminated in the evening, adds to the charm of the area. Travelling across Jones Bridge offers a glimpse into Manila\'s rich history and cultural heritage, making it a memorable part of exploring the city.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-06-02T18:11:36.384841188Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:3;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:26:\"Maria Carla Villete (caca)\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:121:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIsQC30q1GHZKLqNBY57T9yFGQlpY2xNODE6rwVrNiG9ZKsZmQ=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba4\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:573:\"it’s really nice to walk around and check every stalls has to offer, although the smell from the river bothers me a bit too much that all the things that has been coming out of my mouth were complains ? (i have sensitive sense of smell)\n\ntoo bad my partner is not inviting me to come here anymore (maybe he got concerned about me getting uncomfy with the smell and this could ruin a lot of things T-T) but the place is really great, well maintained, entertaining, and a very fun and memorable experience! such a nice place to end your manila trip/travel itinerary ?\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-02-12T12:09:06.225459839Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:4;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:8:\"Q Asmbrd\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:123:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjUs5vf_ae8-8z7BW6ydsa-votm3jkQAY4xs4rYWm2ySlsvNfSFw=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba6\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:258:\"When you though it was just a normal bridge but it actually has a nice architectural design and if you just use different mental filter when looking at it, it was amusing. In this view, it felt like we are not in Binondo. It felt like we are some place else.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-01-30T05:08:52.835007622Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}}s:17:\"editorial_summary\";N;s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"google\";}i:13;a:15:{s:2:\"id\";s:27:\"ChIJAZFEnybKlzMRzVWj2vJ_B7M\";s:4:\"name\";s:34:\"National Museum of Natural History\";s:3:\"lat\";d:14.5833236;s:3:\"lng\";d:120.98219359999999;s:7:\"address\";s:71:\"Teodoro F. Valencia Cir, Ermita, Manila, 1000 Metro Manila, Philippines\";s:8:\"category\";s:12:\"google_place\";s:4:\"type\";s:8:\"landmark\";s:6:\"rating\";d:4.7;s:18:\"user_ratings_total\";i:6227;s:7:\"reviews\";i:6227;s:15:\"photo_reference\";s:477:\"places/ChIJAZFEnybKlzMRzVWj2vJ_B7M/photos/AVoNoXRImyyRrL7Pe5s9Atb9nJEBgS2jAAmTL0Yl0Uv9cac-rHSLf74tF8uPlwkGqS0Aciozn-WMHA9KG9cduDDVsUQka0zRrMrWf8vFmt9IyGWxKrSCIOVJq-Sq1eK5TmZzO3rxL646IL9JaUN8mMtR6petdY5mCc5xgY-veFOzUsKTyp1K39TWqZ3z9Rhh9JtAR7kr0_AYHTyxiilSYoa_8D9aHuz9ERRixOzSY3bCIU-Ma4rb2wt_KR6GFzvuzX6C3u2Bv5_JsOD8AF1jEMHzTJzfUNHs97DRHchEtbFZwdAoS37MbY75LMCLhpNHTdwzhvkj3y3cqhAWoiFq3JUEm5BS9aAMGxECfBnmuzTEZwOsJTI-IbadboHpixxo9flvwHedRDB