 explore if you want to try different Chinese-Filipino dishes and street food. Walking around the area feels like a mix of history and food trip at the same time. Definitely a must-visit if you’re in Manila and love trying new food.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-03-10T08:52:05.019779012Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:2;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:15:\"romelyn dueñas\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:125:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjX8-OUWlHsfA6-wR1_3tLTdih4kgrDW5sk0usCYXXistmI6_M4Gng=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba4\";s:6:\"rating\";i:3;s:4:\"text\";s:1199:\"I\'ve been to Manila China Town many times now, and I would say it\'s yay and nay place to visit.\n\nIn terms of food, you got to be wise. Filipinos love to try the hype and trendy ones, so you might catch yourself waiting in line at Cafe Mezzanine or Eng Bee Tin. But ask yourself if they are really worth the hype? Btw, there are Eng Bee Tin stalls in various malls like MOA and Ayala, so better get them there. Cafe Mezzanine is just right there, close to Manila China Town sign, so it gets crowded very quickly.\n\nI\'d like to walk further from the crowd and dine at YingYing Tea House. One of the most renowed yet still very welcoming, affordable, with decent amout of serving  Such a great place to stuff yourself after walking.\n\nSpeaking of walking, this is not a place for high heels, and uncomfy shoes. Ready yourself to gather some steps as you move from one ally to another.\n\nThe town is a mixture of retail businesses, and squatters living in abandoned buildings. You\'ll be surprised to be in a sketchy place in a second.\n\nSo, just be extra careful esp. for solo travellers, do it in day time.\n\nOne more thing, if you\'re taking the LRT, make sure get off at Carriedo Station.\n\nEnjoy your trip!\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-03-29T03:05:28.212121054Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:3;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:18:\"Herra Mae G. Rubio\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:125:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjVeetnf55Hebc9GBH2Ta56fPW0KvZ4JbxZ8uC0Sg37BeRhNBFtDLQ=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba3\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:268:\"Excellent place for a quick stroll and food lovers.\n\nI won\'t recommend to go there late at night (8 PM onwards) since most of the stalls and restos were already closed. The streets has colorful lights you can capture with your cams. Street foods are available as well.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-03-25T10:42:52.603962766Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:4;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:9:\"Dailycent\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:121:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjUfuZ50QIHzD2bM0Hl81LkZto260VoBpBNtdUUPFVn3jGZaCA=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:311:\"Walking through Binondo Chinatown feels personal every street has a story, every food stop feels earned. The flavors are authentic and bold, the kind you only get from places that have been doing it right for generations. It is lively, real, and full of character no rush, just good food and culture all around.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-02-25T13:47:15.094441371Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}}s:17:\"editorial_summary\";N;s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"google\";}i:17;a:15:{s:2:\"id\";s:27:\"ChIJ4XE4W9nLlzMRZ4i18ka8zt4\";s:4:\"name\";s:42:\"Philippine International Convention Center\";s:3:\"lat\";d:14.554498299999997;s:3:\"lng\";d:120.98303319999998;s:7:\"address\";s:51:\"PICC Complex, Pasay City, Metro Manila, Philippines\";s:8:\"category\";s:12:\"google_place\";s:4:\"type\";s:8:\"landmark\";s:6:\"rating\";d:4.6;s:18:\"user_ratings_total\";i:8039;s:7:\"reviews\";i:8039;s:15:\"photo_reference\";s:478:\"places/ChIJ4XE4W9nLlzMRZ4i18ka8zt4/photos/AVoNoXTi79PXAGO611znKVEplyQ93kPCeyog6o7p7BX5UyPbDutM27SC3DoZ26fcKHxP584nUw9_HPp16uLlHK7-qagn1QCZJ-vH0GLIh5XuQp4GuClKr9ePnYiZ_q2iiOb5U0Fn0EUkYbAwGRnab3k9sYtuFEl3SiVfsAqx-IvdtceQlZo-FLIns30w4Qo6YB9qsDoXoQrAUBsaXva9VGmxDR05SWR-ZLrF6hBs8bX9cX2GyBbFH8FpHDHTD5w6IWlHybvYX9YxtCd8-veXa_Ih4gu8_G54hg4DJykUYJANAHOrGQ0wJYTth77W4gb5ZgCMHvZNvxRlEyrGA